WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.078
Quantum computers are increasingly, you know, coming along and,

00:00:03.078 --> 00:00:06.840
you know, with new breakthroughs and new papers coming every week.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:11.975
It's not fair to say it's the quantum safe coin because it's not actually safe against

00:00:11.975 --> 00:00:13.900
quantum computers as it currently stands.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.240
I was like an OG Bitcoiner. What we're building here is something that is planetary for

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.280
like hundreds of millions of users. This is a dangerous, risky technology.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:26.440
I don't really separate money into store of value and medium of exchange.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.160
I think it's like two sides of the same coin.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.380
The price is going up. Everyone says, don't change anything and ruin it.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:35.537
Don't ruin it. Don't touch it. And when the price is going down,

00:00:35.537 --> 00:00:40.620
everyone says, let's do something. You are a Cypherpunk advisor.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.440
Why join Cypherpunk? Why be an advisor?

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:49.940
Well, they invited me to be an advisor and it seemed like a good idea at the time.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.240
I thought Cypherpunk was going to be a DAT.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.820
And even then, I thought a DAT was probably a good idea.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:04.002
It would give various parties the ability to get price exposure to ZEK that they otherwise

00:01:04.002 --> 00:01:07.480
couldn't get because of their organizational rules or structure.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.320
Yeah, for institutions, it's very useful.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.920
And I trust Tyler and Cameron's intentions.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:21.235
They have the right goals that I share. And I get Cypherpunk stock options or some kind

00:01:21.235 --> 00:01:27.820
of pay, some kind of indirect pay for doing it.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:34.035
So I agreed to be an advisor to Cypherpunk, which is one of only basically two professional

00:01:34.035 --> 00:01:36.960
obligations I've agreed to do at this point.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.800
The chief product officer at Shielded Labs and an advisor to Cypherpunk.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:47.935
One of the big topics that's been, you know, the main, the current thing in the Zcash

00:01:47.935 --> 00:01:52.964
ecosystem has been quantum computing. As you may be aware, quantum computers are increasingly,

00:01:52.964 --> 00:01:57.820
you know, coming along and, you know, with new breakthroughs and new papers.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:02.611
Coming every week. Some have, like, positioned Zcash as, like,

00:02:02.611 --> 00:02:07.460
the quantum safe coin. Do you have any thoughts on that?

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:12.240
Like, what's your perspective of, like, Zcash's position on the quantum problem?

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.956
It's not fair to say it's the quantum safe coin because it's not actually safe against

00:02:17.956 --> 00:02:20.100
quantum computers as it currently stands.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:27.100
The best thing about Zcash is that there's a culture and a tradition of...

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:33.431
Responsible innovation. Yeah. Like, we don't move fast and break things,

00:02:33.431 --> 00:02:36.666
but nor do we, like, try to hold absolutely still.

00:02:36.666 --> 00:02:41.928
Yeah, which is very important. And Zcash has a lot of really good cryptographers and

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:47.400
security engineers who've been thinking about this since long before it became the current thing.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:56.380
And we've got some defenses against certain kinds of problems already built in.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:01.343
And some of the other cryptographers, not me and not Sheldon Labs,

00:03:01.343 --> 00:03:07.960
but some of the other teams working on Zcash have developed a roadmap to further protect

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:12.509
the users against quantum computers over the coming years in a,

00:03:12.509 --> 00:03:17.472
like, responsible, incremental fashion that doesn't disrupt or put everything at unnecessary

00:03:17.472 --> 00:03:21.640
risk, but also makes progress. Yeah. So that's what I like about Zcash.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:26.220
But it's overselling to say that Zcash is the quantum safe.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:31.120
It's the quantum safe coin, because it's currently not...

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.046
No major coins currently are. Yeah. And they've all started,

00:03:35.046 --> 00:03:39.308
like, trying to move that direction, or at least all of them except Bitcoin have started

00:03:39.308 --> 00:03:40.640
trying to move that direction.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:45.860
Yeah, we're not there yet. I think what's promising about Zcash is there's a lot of work

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:48.100
being done about it. It's taken very seriously.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:53.555
If a quantum solution is put forward, my guess is that it would likely be adopted just

00:03:53.555 --> 00:03:57.702
because of the culture of Zcash. Whereas you could argue that for Bitcoin,

00:03:57.702 --> 00:04:01.222
that is much less certain due to a lot of, like, you know, divisiveness in,

00:04:01.222 --> 00:04:03.480
like, the Bitcoin community. Do you have any thoughts on that?

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.020
Yeah, I agree. Let me put it positively.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:11.720
I have this practice I'm practicing, which is don't say what's bad about a bad thing.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.920
Instead, say what's good about a good thing.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.660
For one thing, double negatives confuse me.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.080
But also, it makes everyone focus better.

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:24.860
So what's good, I'm thinking about bad things about Bitcoin.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.160
Yeah. But let me express. Let me express those good things about Zcash.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:33.487
So, A, like we just said, the Zcash community is willing to evolve,

00:04:33.487 --> 00:04:38.840
like, at the right pace, carefully and responsibly, but with intention.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:44.593
Yep. And the other thing that you just mentioned is that the Zcash community is largely

00:04:44.593 --> 00:04:46.020
more, like, win-win oriented.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:51.050
Yep. Like, we may not agree on everything, but we have a lot of shared values and shared

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:56.020
goals, and we can cooperate. I think that's really valuable long run for...

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:03.326
For building. There's a concept I saw a lot with the original,

00:05:03.326 --> 00:05:07.698
with the Bitcoin culture. I don't know if you know, but I was,

00:05:07.698 --> 00:05:10.261
like, an OG Bitcoiner. I know, I know. I don't know if you know,

00:05:10.261 --> 00:05:12.820
but I'm the author of the first blog post ever written about Bitcoin.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:18.708
I know, I know. And there was, there developed a culture there of,

00:05:18.708 --> 00:05:26.000
it sort of, like, took the network level and cryptographic level principles that you...

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.318
You want to minimize trust, right? You want to, you don't want to be vulnerable or to

00:05:31.318 --> 00:05:34.180
take the word of another node over the network.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:38.600
Instead, you want to be able to verify everything and sort of defend yourself as a node.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:43.560
Yep. And for whatever reason, people also applied that to the human level.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.760
Like, you're not going to trust any other humans, and you're going to distrust them.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:55.220
It's like the culture in which, sort of, bad faith is, should be the assumption.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:06:02.071
Yes. The assumption is bad faith. And that was, a lot of people thought of that as,

00:06:02.071 --> 00:06:07.690
they would say, like, Bitcoin is so important that we can't be,

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:10.500
like, namby-pamby-ing around with being nice.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.840
It's so important that we need to be mean.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:17.720
And I always thought, yeah, but, like, being nice works better.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.160
Like, it's so important. You want to be, you want to get stuff done.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:25.760
Yeah. And human trust and cooperativeness and focusing on the world.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.040
Win-win opportunities get stuff done in a way that distrust doesn't.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.760
Yeah. I guess we just got onto that, because you mentioned something about the Zcash culture.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:40.976
I agree. I agree. I think one of the big positives, I think we were speaking of that

00:06:40.976 --> 00:06:43.740
earlier, for Zcash is really the culture, right?

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.751
This openness to innovation. I mean, there's still, like, you know,

00:06:47.751 --> 00:06:53.560
a conservative aspect, which I think is very important for store of value.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:07:00.590
But the fact... The fact that we are open to innovation and open to the ideas of others

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:06.037
is very important for quantum, for maybe at some points potentially replacing proof-of-work

00:07:06.037 --> 00:07:11.160
for, like, these types of things. So, yeah, I agree.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.120
Yeah. Yeah, the world is never going to stop changing.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.000
Yeah. The needs of the people are never going to stop changing.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:24.520
I agree. Speaking of proof-of-work, so, like, one of the major focuses of Shielded Labs

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:29.761
is Crosslink. Maybe let's first cover what Crosslink is, because I think,

00:07:29.761 --> 00:07:34.251
like, the proposal has, like, changed quite a bit since, like,

00:07:34.251 --> 00:07:36.700
the original first ideas came about.

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:40.921
And then we can, you know, go over, like, you know, the case for it,

00:07:40.921 --> 00:07:44.580
some of the critiques, and, yeah, discuss more about it, if that's okay.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:51.593
Crosslink is a proposal to change the Zcash consensus algorithm by keeping the current

00:07:51.593 --> 00:07:56.080
proof-of-work in place and adding a finality gadget.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:00.828
Yep. So I kind of think metaphorically, like, proof-of-work,

00:08:00.828 --> 00:08:04.860
which was Satoshi's big breakthrough contribution, right?

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:10.785
It's kind of like an engine, a self-powering engine that's always pushing forward,

00:08:10.785 --> 00:08:16.127
which is amazing and very useful. And then the finality gadget,

00:08:16.127 --> 00:08:25.660
which used proof-of-state technology to make the finality gadget work.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:27.720
It prevents backsliding. Okay?

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:35.580
Proof-of-work by itself doesn't have a notion of when a thing is permanent.

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:41.180
It can always be undone by the engine pushing in a new direction.

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:45.900
It's probabilistic. Right. Well, that's what people say.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:55.640
And I actually think, at, like, a mathematical level, that's a misnomer.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:03.020
I wouldn't think about security as probabilistic.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:09.932
If it's worth enough to the attacker, if they have the opportunity and they have the

00:09:09.932 --> 00:09:17.345
means and the motive to take advantage of you, it is just going to confuse you and mislead

00:09:17.345 --> 00:09:23.220
you if you think to yourself, there's a probability that they'll take advantage of me.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:24.560
It's not a probability.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:26.640
It's whether they decide to. It's whether they do and whether they can't.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.820
Yes. Yes. Yes. Correct. Yeah. Yeah. That's always a risk.

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:34.135
I mean, and we've seen, like, these reorg attacks, like, for example,

00:09:34.135 --> 00:09:36.600
last week with Litecoin. Yeah. It does happen.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:42.801
But, like, I guess one of, like, the main arguments then brought forward by,

00:09:42.801 --> 00:09:48.560
you know, the other camp is, well, why not just improve proof-of-work currently?

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:52.137
Because there's a lot to improve. Like, I mean, until, I think,

00:09:52.137 --> 00:09:55.600
a month ago, Zcash had just, like, one big miner.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:03.107
Now we have Foundry as well. Is this maybe, like, a first path we should explore before,

00:10:03.107 --> 00:10:04.320
like, exploring Crosslink?

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:07.800
Or are these, like, you know, not mutually exclusive?

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:11.800
Yeah. They're not. I think they're compatible and they help in different ways.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:17.633
There are a bunch of other potential improvements to the proof-of-work component that

00:10:17.633 --> 00:10:23.017
the Valar team is now proposing, like, reducing the times between blocks,

00:10:23.017 --> 00:10:25.260
other kinds of performance improvements.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:30.191
Yeah. And all of those are totally compatible with the Crosslink design because it's

00:10:30.191 --> 00:10:34.664
very modular. Like, Crosslink keeps our current proposal, our design,

00:10:34.664 --> 00:10:39.840
and the prototype that we've implemented, which we're now experimenting with.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:46.200
You can join the channel and play with Crosslink and help us break it.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.560
It's very broken right now.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:52.600
And that's why we do this. Anyway, but the design is extremely modular.

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:55.560
Yeah. Like, any improvements, including the new...

00:10:55.560 --> 00:11:00.233
The new mining pool foundry and the reduced block times and,

00:11:00.233 --> 00:11:05.331
like, improved peer-to-peer networking so that blocks can get communicated and transactions

00:11:05.331 --> 00:11:07.880
can get communicated faster between nodes.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:10.600
All of that is perfectly compatible with Crosslink.

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:17.060
What Crosslink adds is primarily... Let me try to think of the most important things.

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:20.300
It's got, like, quite a few different benefits that I think it offers.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:25.540
But I'd say the two most important ones are irreversible finality.

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:33.388
So that when you can know if someone has deposited a million dollars worth of ZEC into

00:11:33.388 --> 00:11:36.620
your exchange or your DEX, like Near.

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:39.080
So this is what happened to Near. Well...

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:40.880
Yeah, with Litecoin. They actually lost a lot of money.

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:45.600
They lost $600,000, I think, in that event.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:52.436
And it's kind of confusing whether irreversible finality would or wouldn't have fixed

00:11:52.436 --> 00:11:55.680
that in that case. Yeah, because the transaction would have occurred.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:02.625
Yeah. But in any case, in general, if you're operating a DEX or a CEX or you're accepting

00:12:02.625 --> 00:12:07.336
large transactions over the network, irreversible finality tells you that there's not

00:12:07.336 --> 00:12:13.047
a probability here. It's just that once it has the stamp of finality on it,

00:12:13.047 --> 00:12:20.706
you know it cannot be undone. Yeah. That's a much simpler and safer guarantee to reason

00:12:20.706 --> 00:12:22.860
about as an operator.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:25.100
Correct. Correct. Yes, that makes sense.

00:12:25.100 --> 00:12:33.241
Oh, and the other... The second biggest advantage of Crosslink is that it magnifies the

00:12:33.241 --> 00:12:40.678
security budget, in a sense. And we have a more precise technical definition of this

00:12:40.678 --> 00:12:45.380
number, but people call it informally, they call it the security budget.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:52.400
And that's a thing which in Bitcoin and in Zcash, it's tied to the issuance.

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:53.200
Yep. Right?

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:55.860
And the issuance ticks down over time. Of course, yeah.

00:12:55.860 --> 00:13:02.320
And like with Quantum, this is a thing where I think it will be a problem for Bitcoin,

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:06.568
and I don't know if they have the right culture to be willing to make changes to address

00:13:06.568 --> 00:13:12.443
the problem. Yep. And in Zcash, it's got the same problem because Bitcoin and Zcash are

00:13:12.443 --> 00:13:17.960
the two coins that want to maintain a hard, fixed cap on the supply.

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:22.507
Yep. There's only ever 21 million Bitcoins, only ever 21 million Zcash,

00:13:22.507 --> 00:13:25.340
and almost everyone agrees to that. That's the golden rule.

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:28.811
Very few people want to change that, but there's substantial,

00:13:28.811 --> 00:13:35.059
maybe 10% or 20% of all the Zcashers would be happy to change the supply in order to

00:13:35.059 --> 00:13:36.100
gain the sustainability.

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:44.044
Yes. So at Shielded Labs, we have two projects, and one of them is Crosslink,

00:13:44.044 --> 00:13:51.826
which we think can provide the sustainability and thus sort of harden the assurance that

00:13:51.826 --> 00:13:59.356
the supply will remain limited. Yep. Whereas with Ethereum, they long since accepted

00:13:59.356 --> 00:14:04.700
a variable supply in return for various other trade-offs.

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:08.580
And in Bitcoin, I don't know what their plan is.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:13.256
They're committed to the fixed supply, but they don't know how they're going to provide

00:14:13.256 --> 00:14:17.228
the sustainability. Yeah. So two of Shielded Labs' projects,

00:14:17.228 --> 00:14:21.541
Network Sustainability Mechanism and also Crosslink, both contribute to sustainability

00:14:21.541 --> 00:14:24.560
while hardening the 21 million supply cap.

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:25.420
Yeah. That makes sense.

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:29.900
Because like the problem with Bitcoin is, and it's such a logical problem,

00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:34.036
I fail to understand how people cannot see that it's a problem,

00:14:34.036 --> 00:14:38.172
is if you're allocating like a security budget every year to mining,

00:14:38.172 --> 00:14:42.652
which the inflation on Bitcoin is effectively, and you're halving that security budget

00:14:42.652 --> 00:14:46.788
every four years, you need the price to catch up and compensate,

00:14:46.788 --> 00:14:51.608
which at some point it might not. And when it does not, when it doesn't,

00:14:51.608 --> 00:14:54.920
you're going to be in big trouble because your security budget.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:15:01.106
Effectively halves. And so at some points, it's practically guaranteed we'll need like

00:15:01.106 --> 00:15:03.140
an alternative system. Seems like it.

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:06.280
And there's transaction fees. Yes. But those are not enough.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:08.140
But that's also. Like a Bitcoin, it's very little.

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:14.459
Questionable. Yeah. Yes. A thing about Crosslink, one of the two biggest features or

00:15:14.459 --> 00:15:19.619
benefits of Crosslink is that if you take your security budget,

00:15:19.619 --> 00:15:23.840
your issuance, which can come from unissued coins, right?

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:25.640
Because we're still below. Twenty one million.

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:33.434
So we can issue more and it can come from past fees because with shielded labs is network

00:15:33.434 --> 00:15:36.870
sustainability mechanism. Sixty percent of the fees get burned,

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:44.580
so 40% of the fees goes to the current minor of the next block and 60% of the fee gets burned.

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:50.119
And in the network sustainability mechanism, everything that's ever been burned can be

00:15:50.119 --> 00:15:54.380
reincarnated months and years later to add to the sustainability.

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:59.920
But. Maybe a dumb question, but wouldn't that then reduce the security budget for mining?

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:03.020
I mean, I'm guessing that you are then rewarding miners less.

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:09.247
The fee burning or the issuance part? Well, for the fee burning,

00:16:09.247 --> 00:16:12.960
like the fees you're burning previously, I'm assuming they went to miners.

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:15.780
Right. Currently, 100% of the fees go to miners.

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:22.229
Yep. And in the NSM proposal, which is going up for some kind of governance vote shortly,

00:16:22.229 --> 00:16:26.768
but it's already been voted on. It's been voted on once and everyone approved this part

00:16:26.768 --> 00:16:32.800
of it the last time around. But anyway, in that proposal, 60% of each fee gets burned.

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:35.860
That only leaves 40% of the fee for the current miner.

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:41.060
However, that's enough to incentivize them to include that transaction because it's very

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:44.445
cheap for them to add a transaction in. Yeah.

00:16:44.445 --> 00:16:46.300
So your bet is that they will do it anyway.

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:54.440
And they're still motivated to include transactions at the current levels.

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:59.340
Like with Bitcoin, the fees are very little in real money terms.

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:01.300
Oh, yeah. Like it's compared to inflation.

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:08.111
Yeah. But this is, we're very like long term and sustainability and like economic balance

00:17:08.111 --> 00:17:13.792
oriented at Shielded Labs. So almost all of our projects are focused on like if we got

00:17:13.792 --> 00:17:16.500
this into place, got everyone to agree to it.

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:19.100
Yep. And then the world radically changed.

00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:21.880
How would the network do in that situation?

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:24.300
Yes. Yes. Yeah. Yeah. And I think it is.

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:29.491
I think it is very important to plan for like the future where like what we're building

00:17:29.491 --> 00:17:33.460
here is something that is planetary for like hundreds of millions of users.

00:17:33.460 --> 00:17:36.327
That's the plan. That's the goal. We don't want to, you know,

00:17:36.327 --> 00:17:39.462
like it's nice to have our little community of like, you know,

00:17:39.462 --> 00:17:43.380
a few hundreds, maybe a few thousands of people who are very committed to Zcash.

00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:48.020
But the end goal is millions of users, millions of transactions every day.

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:53.060
And yeah. Which increase the fees, increase the load.

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:59.758
Yes. Yes. And increase all the sort of like stress on makers of software and services

00:17:59.758 --> 00:18:04.706
and everything. Correct. And those hundreds of millions or however many users,

00:18:04.706 --> 00:18:07.520
they make it much harder to change things.

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:13.179
Yes. Yes. Yes. Yes. I'm hoping to get these things in now because I think they're like

00:18:13.179 --> 00:18:15.260
long term. Because later on it will be much harder.

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:17.980
Yeah. Probably. Hopefully. Yeah. Hopefully.

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:19.420
Hopefully. Indeed. Indeed. Indeed. Indeed.

00:18:19.420 --> 00:18:22.560
I guess to close on the crosslink chapter.

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:23.860
Like what are the other?

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:26.280
The critiques. I think Mert has given his critique.

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:32.976
I myself do resonate with it as well is that it might make the protocol too complex or

00:18:32.976 --> 00:18:36.440
too complicated. Yeah. Because you're introducing a lot of like new variables.

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:39.000
Like proof of work is great because it's pretty simple.

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:41.760
Yeah. Like it's really just very simple.

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:44.700
Turns out. Like the miners guess like an ounce.

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:46.780
Turns out to be pretty complicated in practice.

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:49.960
But yes. Even simple things turn out to be really complicated.

00:18:49.960 --> 00:18:53.860
So I really resonate too with that factor.

00:18:53.860 --> 00:18:59.020
That critique that like complexity is definitely the enemy especially for store value.

00:18:59.020 --> 00:19:03.544
Yeah. Yeah. Right. Because we want confidence, reliability, safety,

00:19:03.544 --> 00:19:10.320
even in face of attack, attempted exploitation, all kinds of attacks from different parties.

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:15.200
So I totally agree with that. There's an aspect of our current crosslink design,

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:19.620
which is kind of targeted that way. I'm not saying they're wrong to critique it.

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:22.612
Like it's definitely two things is way more complicated than one,

00:19:22.612 --> 00:19:25.020
like more than twice. It's complicated. Right.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:33.140
Like two things is a lot, but there's an aspect of it, which I'm pretty satisfied with.

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:37.767
And we solicited some sort of independent analysis from economics,

00:19:37.767 --> 00:19:43.320
mechanism design to try to double check if we're getting this right.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:47.580
But there are different components and each component only has one job.

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:53.540
Yeah. And so in a way that simplifies things like in the current in Bitcoin.

00:19:53.540 --> 00:20:01.186
And in Zcash currently, the proof of work miners, people are counting on them both to

00:20:01.186 --> 00:20:06.920
keep making progress and including transactions and not to backslide too much.

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:10.360
Yeah. So they kind of have two sort of competing goals.

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:17.336
Yes. And then they normally do this well, but of course it gets more complicated when

00:20:17.336 --> 00:20:23.380
due to load due to like bugs that are coming out from the cyber.

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:30.300
Security, AI, wave, things like that, the complications compound.

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:34.575
Yeah. And a thing that I really like about the current crosslink design is now there's

00:20:34.575 --> 00:20:36.840
two components, but each one only has one job.

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:41.754
Yeah. The proof of work miners can't backslide or undo anybody's transaction,

00:20:41.754 --> 00:20:47.772
even if they tried, and they don't need to worry about that because the finalizers,

00:20:47.772 --> 00:20:53.800
it's called validators in other blockchains.

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:56.600
But in crosslink, they don't validate anything.

00:20:56.600 --> 00:21:02.284
All they do is put the stamp of approval that this thing cannot be undone,

00:21:02.284 --> 00:21:03.800
the irreversible stamp, okay?

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:09.496
And so the finalizers, they just have one job, just put the irreversible stamp on things

00:21:09.496 --> 00:21:13.180
and never undo it. They, as a component, they can be simpler.

00:21:13.180 --> 00:21:17.920
They're not responsible for including transactions, dealing with all the other like issues.

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:20.300
Yeah, they do one thing, they do it well, you know.

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:23.140
Yeah. So I agree, complexity is the hard part.

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:27.020
And we're always fighting against complexity at all levels.

00:21:27.020 --> 00:21:31.111
Let's move forward to a new topic. Like you were speaking about,

00:21:31.111 --> 00:21:36.340
you know, the other proposals of Shielded Labs being NSM and dynamic fees.

00:21:36.340 --> 00:21:38.800
Any update on those?

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:44.830
My understanding is that both were put forward in the last NU7,

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:48.560
I think, voting proposal. I think the voting was confused.

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:55.260
Okay. Because the actual ballot said, for each of, there was many questions,

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:57.820
like 12 or something, different questions, or 10.

00:21:57.820 --> 00:22:04.339
And the ballot said things like, do you generally like the idea of fees being based on

00:22:04.339 --> 00:22:06.640
market conditions or adapting or whatever?

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:12.512
Yeah. But then, sort of the accompanying extra information said,

00:22:12.512 --> 00:22:20.464
this question is about this specific protocol in a draft Zcash improvement proposal in

00:22:20.464 --> 00:22:25.840
like GitHub. So now it's ambiguous. Are you voting for and against the general idea or

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:28.780
are you voting for or against this specific preliminary protocol?

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:35.325
Yeah. Anyway, a bunch of the, like, interest groups, there's a voting body that's all

00:22:35.325 --> 00:22:41.803
the core devs, and there's like the Zcash Brazil community, and then there's like the

00:22:41.803 --> 00:22:46.985
Zcash Foundation's longstanding advisory panel that's been doing this for like eight

00:22:46.985 --> 00:22:51.280
years or whatever. Yeah. I think they all voted in favor of the dynamic fees.

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:55.120
They all voted in favor of the dynamic fees item.

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:59.160
And then 80% of the coin holders voted against the dynamic fees item.

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:07.392
Okay. And one guy, Dave from the Valar group, posted his rationale on Twitter as to how

00:23:07.392 --> 00:23:12.116
he, why he voted how he voted. He voted against dynamic fees,

00:23:12.116 --> 00:23:18.320
saying the reason was he disagreed with some specific detail of the draft protocol.

00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:22.580
Anyway, I don't know. I don't know if there's going to be another vote.

00:23:22.580 --> 00:23:26.160
Yeah. Some, like, other people are organizing that stuff.

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:29.841
I think there's also, like, still a lot of room for, like, the,

00:23:29.841 --> 00:23:33.229
you know, voting to be improved. Whether it being available in,

00:23:33.229 --> 00:23:37.282
you know, in Soddle and Zyngo and other Zcash wallets, but also,

00:23:37.282 --> 00:23:41.673
obviously, like, the questions should be, like, very clear and simple so,

00:23:41.673 --> 00:23:46.740
you know, everyone knows what they're actually voting on and what they're not voting on.

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:50.080
Right. Or at least they're all voting on the same question as each other for starters.

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:55.350
Yeah. Because, yeah, in this case, for example, it's very hard to actually learn actionable

00:23:55.350 --> 00:23:59.660
information from it because we actually don't know what the reasoning was behind it.

00:23:59.660 --> 00:24:03.160
Yeah. Like, if actually people understood the right, like, the question.

00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:05.960
Yeah. Yeah, and which question, as you said, they voted on.

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:07.660
Yeah, it'd be interesting if there was some process.

00:24:07.660 --> 00:24:11.120
I don't know. I'm speculating here. It's not really my department,

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:17.392
but it'd be cool if there was something that were more deliberative or people had to

00:24:17.392 --> 00:24:20.920
post their rationales along with their votes or something.

00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:25.103
Then we'd learn a lot more because just a yay/nay vote on a specific question could be

00:24:25.103 --> 00:24:30.322
interpreted a lot of different ways. Yeah, or maybe, as I think you had mentioned in

00:24:30.322 --> 00:24:34.813
the earlier discussion, have sub-questions where it's yes to this specific outcome,

00:24:34.813 --> 00:24:38.929
no to this specific, like, yes to this other specific outcome,

00:24:38.929 --> 00:24:43.420
or, like, just no, kill it and let's just not do this.

00:24:43.420 --> 00:24:46.362
For example, like, you know, for issuance curves, like, this curve,

00:24:46.362 --> 00:24:49.840
this curve, or just no curve at all, like, we don't change anything.

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:52.833
Yeah. I think that, you know, that would be way more actionable and,

00:24:52.833 --> 00:24:54.140
yeah, just way more useful. Makes sense.

00:24:54.140 --> 00:24:59.782
And this is an example of, like, responding to the previous vote by asking a more specific

00:24:59.782 --> 00:25:04.761
or clarifying question or seeing if some people are going to shift and converge between

00:25:04.761 --> 00:25:11.108
then and now or something. So it's more, I don't know, an interactive process than a

00:25:11.108 --> 00:25:17.157
one-shot vote. Yeah. I think you had some conversations with Daira Emma about coin-weighted

00:25:17.157 --> 00:25:20.680
voting, and I think she was generally against it.

00:25:20.680 --> 00:25:24.600
You were in favor? I don't remember this conversation last year.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:27.520
Oh, too bad. Was this on the forum? I'm guessing, yeah.

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:30.980
Like, what's your position right now on coin-weighted voting?

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:33.600
Like, what's, you know, are you in favor of it?

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:41.571
Well, historically, there was a time when most people who were active in the Zcash community

00:25:41.571 --> 00:25:46.560
were against it for valid reasons. One of them was Vitalik Buterin,

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:50.560
who was always a Zcacher in addition to being an Ethereum person.

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:54.560
And others were like Peter Van Valkenburg, who's now, at the time,

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:57.560
he was on the Zcash Foundation Board of Directors.

00:25:57.560 --> 00:26:02.220
He subsequently stepped down from that in order to become the executive director of Coin Center.

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:07.455
Yeah, that makes sense, yeah. But Vitalik and Peter and others expressed concerns about,

00:26:07.455 --> 00:26:11.220
like, the risks or downsides of coin-weighted voting.

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:18.345
And my position at the time was, well, yeah, but those are risks if the coin holders

00:26:18.345 --> 00:26:22.440
have, like, all of the... Yeah....power.

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:25.440
Yeah. All of the decision. And currently, they have, like, zero power or zero voice at all.

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:31.440
Yeah. Like, nobody even knows what they think, much less will give them any credence.

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:35.849
Yes, yeah. And my position at the time was, so, I think they should have somewhere between,

00:26:35.849 --> 00:26:37.440
like, one-thirds and two-thirds of the power.

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:41.080
Yeah. And then it's probably not going to run into those, like,

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:44.440
total risks on that side and probably not the total risks on this side.

00:26:44.440 --> 00:26:49.796
Yeah. This is typical of me to figure, like, the extremes are more risky...

00:26:49.796 --> 00:26:52.320
Yeah....and the middle ground is probably safer sometimes.

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:57.453
Yeah. Okay. Well, I still think that. I still think coin holders should have between

00:26:57.453 --> 00:26:59.320
one-thirds and two-thirds of the political power.

00:26:59.320 --> 00:27:03.320
Yeah. It's totally unclear to me how much they currently have.

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:07.320
I think it's technically a third, but, like, it's all in just,

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:08.320
like, culture, right?

00:27:08.320 --> 00:27:11.320
Like, I mean... Right. There's no strict rule.

00:27:11.320 --> 00:27:15.070
Like, there's a funny thing about political power is if you convince someone you have

00:27:15.070 --> 00:27:19.320
it and there's no point... Yes....in resisting you, then you have it.

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:25.733
Yeah. Yeah. Now, the one technical thing that I can tell without trying to infer a culture...

00:27:25.733 --> 00:27:30.200
Yeah....is that coin holders control, I think it's 60% of the funding.

00:27:30.200 --> 00:27:35.262
Yeah. Because 60% of the funding goes to retroactive grants determined solely by the

00:27:35.262 --> 00:27:40.867
coin holder votes. Yeah. 40% of the funding goes to other kinds of grants controlled

00:27:40.867 --> 00:27:43.200
solely by the Zcash Community Grants Committee.

00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:47.800
Yeah. I think of the 20% that is going to the DEF Fund, it's 12% that's going to one

00:27:47.800 --> 00:27:49.200
and 8% are going to the other.

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:52.080
Exactly. Yeah, that's correct. Same numbers.

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:56.080
Yeah, same numbers. So that fits nicely within my one-third to two-third range.

00:27:56.080 --> 00:28:02.413
Yeah. One of the, you know, aside from Crosslink, one of the other hot topics in the

00:28:02.413 --> 00:28:08.080
Zcash Community has been Tachyon, which, you know, has been for a lot of people very exciting.

00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:11.080
Like, you know, the promise of, like, near infinite scalability.

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:17.080
Yeah. Any thoughts or updated thoughts on Tachyon?

00:28:17.080 --> 00:28:22.960
First of all, I love the idea. Yeah. I love the founder, Sean Bowe.

00:28:22.960 --> 00:28:28.460
And it's not the first time he's come up with a breakthrough idea that seemed… Oh,

00:28:28.460 --> 00:28:33.960
no. Hello, too. Yeah. …literally sort of incomprehensible to other people at first.

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:35.960
Like, Sean was the only one who got it at first.

00:28:35.960 --> 00:28:39.960
Yeah. Yeah. Tachyon is very much like that for me.

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:43.960
It's kind of hard to even understand how that's possible or how it would work.

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:47.400
So I'm really excited. And in addition to coming up with these breakthrough ideas,

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:49.840
Sean is one of very few people that I've met.

00:28:49.840 --> 00:28:56.840
Two people who both, the Venn diagram of people who come up with novel scientific breakthroughs

00:28:56.840 --> 00:29:01.840
and the people who engineer stuff until it works and ship it has a very small sliver

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:02.840
of a Venn diagram.

00:29:02.840 --> 00:29:05.840
Yeah. Yeah. So that's all very encouraging.

00:29:05.840 --> 00:29:12.920
I definitely think Tachyon, like Crosslink and, like, all these changes that we've been

00:29:12.920 --> 00:29:20.720
talking about, it's going to be a really good test of the Zcash culture of… Yeah.

00:29:20.720 --> 00:29:23.720
…responsible evolution. Yeah. Responsible upgrade.

00:29:23.720 --> 00:29:30.720
It's going to be very difficult to get all the users and all the software implementations

00:29:30.720 --> 00:29:35.680
and everyone to cooperate, to deploy a new thing while, you know,

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:40.720
maintaining a sufficient level of stability and safety for all the current users,

00:29:40.720 --> 00:29:44.237
etc., etc. Especially because my understanding is with wallets,

00:29:44.237 --> 00:29:49.100
with Tachyon, wallets will need to be completely, I mean, rebuilt,

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:50.480
essentially. Right?

00:29:50.480 --> 00:29:55.660
I mean, they need… I think… I don't… I haven't thought that much about it,

00:29:55.660 --> 00:29:58.840
but yeah, my instinct is, yeah, the… Everything changes.

00:29:58.840 --> 00:30:01.640
…everything about the… And the wallet is sort of the focal point,

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:02.140
right?

00:30:02.140 --> 00:30:05.605
Yeah. Yeah. Like, the… It's kind of like the wallet is the center of the universe and

00:30:05.605 --> 00:30:07.840
the blockchain is just one of these peripheral pieces… Yeah.

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:12.840
…that serves the wallet. Yeah. Yeah. But I think one of the exciting things with Tachyon

00:30:12.840 --> 00:30:19.480
is it will have sufficient… It will have the ability to make post-quantum Zcash scalable.

00:30:19.480 --> 00:30:23.980
Because, you know, with post-quantum cryptography is very heavy,

00:30:23.980 --> 00:30:29.918
it will make the transactions way bigger, which means that if we don't change anything

00:30:29.918 --> 00:30:34.584
in terms of scalability, we will be able to do even fewer transactions than we are able

00:30:34.584 --> 00:30:37.360
to do today. Right. So, yeah.

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:39.800
Any thoughts on that? Like, where, you know, Tachyon can help?

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:42.480
I may misunderstand some of this stuff. Yeah.

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:45.440
Like, Tachyon is not my… I'm just like a fanboy of Tachyon, right?

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:48.360
My jobs at Shielded Labs include cross-link… Yeah.

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:53.680
Yeah. …monetizationism, dynamic fees, and supporting a bunch of mining pools and CEXs

00:30:53.680 --> 00:30:56.760
and DEXs to, like, help them not get hacked.

00:30:56.760 --> 00:31:02.600
Yeah. So, I don't like to sound off on things that are not my business… Yeah.

00:31:02.600 --> 00:31:04.600
Yeah, of course. …get in other people's way.

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:13.240
Yeah. Tachyon moves a bunch of state management and transmission of data… Yes.

00:31:13.240 --> 00:31:15.240
…off of the blockchain. So, that's great.

00:31:15.240 --> 00:31:18.173
That's a huge improvement, although it immediately raises the question of,

00:31:18.173 --> 00:31:21.440
okay, then who does all that state management… Yeah.

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:23.440
Yeah. …and transmission?

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:27.122
But even if we can… If the Tachyon team can solve all that, we can get it deployed,

00:31:27.122 --> 00:31:33.952
get all the wallets or some wallets to support it, that extra bandwidth can be used for

00:31:33.952 --> 00:31:40.600
post-quantum signatures and encryption, but that doesn't protect against a quantum computer

00:31:40.600 --> 00:31:45.991
counterfeiting Zcash, which is almost like the… would be… if you could just pick one

00:31:45.991 --> 00:31:50.120
thing to protect… to prevent quantum computers for doing… Yeah.

00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:52.120
Yeah. …preventing them from counterfeiting Zcash would be a great pick.

00:31:52.120 --> 00:31:58.120
Yeah. Yeah. I agree. I agree. It's the thing they would probably want to do most if… Yes.

00:31:58.120 --> 00:31:59.120
…if they could do stuff to you.

00:31:59.120 --> 00:32:03.402
Yeah. Yeah. And it makes sense the way the Tachyon team and the Valar team,

00:32:03.402 --> 00:32:06.192
of what little I understand of how they've been sketching out this roadmap,

00:32:06.192 --> 00:32:11.320
is to sort of do the things that we know how to do now, that we could do sooner,

00:32:11.320 --> 00:32:14.415
and the things that protect privacy against future quantum attackers,

00:32:14.415 --> 00:32:19.280
because if they want to violate your privacy, they can look back in time to violate your privacy.

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:21.280
Yeah. So those are really good things to schedule first.

00:32:21.280 --> 00:32:27.280
Yeah. But it's like the end of the roadmap is sort of like a big question mark on the

00:32:27.280 --> 00:32:28.280
last step.

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:31.280
Yes. And now we invent a way to prevent quantum computers from counterfeiting Zcash.

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:35.280
Yeah. And Tachyon doesn't help with that as far as I understand.

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:39.280
My understanding is that's also like a much harder problem, which will… I think so.

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:41.280
…which will take probably, yeah, I'm guessing years.

00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:47.730
You know, speaking of the initiatives of Shiloh Labs, I think you mentioned Shiloh Labs

00:32:47.730 --> 00:32:50.960
has recently been helping miners, exchanges, etc.

00:32:50.960 --> 00:32:55.160
To, you know, run on Zcash, build on Zcash.

00:32:55.160 --> 00:32:57.700
You know, can you say more about that?

00:32:57.700 --> 00:33:02.725
We are offering like professional support services, and like,

00:33:02.725 --> 00:33:07.580
it's like enterprise software support, right?

00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:12.175
So we're prioritizing companies that are willing and able to pay us to do this,

00:33:12.175 --> 00:33:16.040
so we can afford to spend all the time to do it.

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:21.040
And our dream… And I don't… I don't know how, like, if these companies are going to want

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:26.180
to do this, and how well it will work for all the other orgs in the Zcash ecosystem,

00:33:26.180 --> 00:33:30.908
but I imagine it'll be good for all the orgs that Shiloh Labs becomes the point of contact

00:33:30.908 --> 00:33:34.999
for like, you know, staying up all night responding to security concerns,

00:33:34.999 --> 00:33:39.700
and then other people can focus on their building instead.

00:33:39.700 --> 00:33:45.048
Okay. So we have like three more questions to go, so first question is,

00:33:45.048 --> 00:33:53.160
I think last year, two years ago… Yeah. You did a podcast with Arjun Kumani and Josh Swihart.

00:33:53.160 --> 00:33:58.845
In that podcast, at some point, you guys had a disagreement on T-addresses,

00:33:58.845 --> 00:34:02.040
whether or not they should be deprecated.

00:34:02.040 --> 00:34:07.560
My understanding, if I recall correctly, is that Josh was in favor of deprecating them

00:34:07.560 --> 00:34:14.720
and just go fully shielded. I think you liked the optionality of having T-addresses.

00:34:14.720 --> 00:34:16.920
Any updated thoughts on that?

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:22.732
Yeah. Well, this is also not one of my jobs at Shiloh Labs, so I don't want to interfere

00:34:22.732 --> 00:34:27.645
with other people's work, but also it's a really good example of how like my opinions

00:34:27.645 --> 00:34:34.391
or other people's opinions – you can go to the higher level and say the users – the devs

00:34:34.391 --> 00:34:38.480
are not in control. Yeah. Right?

00:34:38.480 --> 00:34:44.583
Which is great. That's cool. Yeah. It's a fascinating, surprising thing about blockchains

00:34:44.583 --> 00:34:48.512
and open permission systems, unlike the previous few decades of software,

00:34:48.512 --> 00:34:53.245
where basically the devs could do anything they wanted and the users had no choice in

00:34:53.245 --> 00:34:55.720
the matter, other than stop using that software.

00:34:55.720 --> 00:35:03.368
Yeah. That was your one option. Yeah. But now, suppose all of the devs – there's a bunch

00:35:03.368 --> 00:35:06.760
of different Zcash development organizations now, which is pretty awesome.

00:35:06.760 --> 00:35:12.449
I'm starting to lose count. There's like six or seven funded,

00:35:12.449 --> 00:35:16.680
skilled, dedicated, Zcash-focused development orgs, which is amazing.

00:35:16.680 --> 00:35:24.626
Wow. Okay, so imagine, hypothetically, all seven or however many got together and said,

00:35:24.626 --> 00:35:30.654
"Let's turn off T-addresses." And suppose – whatever, we can keep going – the Zcash Foundation's

00:35:30.654 --> 00:35:35.260
advisory panel all voted 80% were in favor of turning off Z-addresses.

00:35:35.260 --> 00:35:38.800
The coin holders all voted 80% in favor of turning off T-addresses.

00:35:38.800 --> 00:35:43.895
Okay. Suppose new full nodes and wallet software got uploaded to the App Store,

00:35:43.895 --> 00:35:47.893
and the difference is, when you install this one… When you install this one,

00:35:47.893 --> 00:35:51.560
all your money that is in a T-address is now… Shielded.

00:35:51.560 --> 00:35:54.560
Yeah. No. I was going to say it's now inaccessible.

00:35:54.560 --> 00:35:56.560
Oh, okay. Right?

00:35:56.560 --> 00:35:59.560
Like, you can't… Let's just say, whatever, like… Yeah, hypothetically.

00:35:59.560 --> 00:36:03.532
The easy way to do it is just make T-addresses invalid in the blockchain,

00:36:03.532 --> 00:36:07.600
which effectively means burning everyone's money that's currently in T-addresses.

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:12.040
There's no way to, like, force-shield it all.

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:15.664
So let's just say, for the sake – the point of this story is,

00:36:15.664 --> 00:36:19.360
the devs cannot… Take away a feature from the users, right?

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:23.784
So if we did all that, we went through that process, we uploaded all that software,

00:36:23.784 --> 00:36:27.245
any users who still wanted to keep their money that was currently in a T-address,

00:36:27.245 --> 00:36:30.633
or they still wanted to be able to send and receive with T-addresses,

00:36:30.633 --> 00:36:34.165
they just wouldn't install that software, they'd keep using the software they currently

00:36:34.165 --> 00:36:38.110
have, which currently works for them. Like, I do think there's,

00:36:38.110 --> 00:36:41.884
like, a very important aspect of T-addresses for, like, institutions,

00:36:41.884 --> 00:36:46.768
for exchanges, where, for example, as we were speaking of… Earlier,

00:36:46.768 --> 00:36:51.245
you wouldn't necessarily want an entity like Cypherpunk, for example,

00:36:51.245 --> 00:36:57.520
to have their coins shielded, because that would prevent transparency.

00:36:57.520 --> 00:37:02.080
So users would not be able to audit that Cypherpunk does indeed hold these tokens.

00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:04.820
Yeah, transparency is good for some purposes.

00:37:04.820 --> 00:37:09.924
Yes. And that optional, you know, and especially for exchanges who want to,

00:37:09.924 --> 00:37:14.700
you know, stay within the law, they may be forced to, you know… Well,

00:37:14.700 --> 00:37:27.462
also… The law… Well, also, if they're custodying someone else's funds owned by someone

00:37:27.462 --> 00:37:32.140
else, I mean, I would rather they did that transparently, so we can all watch what they're doing.

00:37:32.140 --> 00:37:35.040
You could have a view key on the other end.

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:40.100
That would be less… That would be sort of a mixture… How would that be better?

00:37:40.100 --> 00:37:46.205
I mean, I guess you could then say, "Oh, we're fully shielded," but if you want to have

00:37:46.205 --> 00:37:47.955
transparency, you can use a view key. It's kind of like the same thing,

00:37:47.955 --> 00:37:50.080
but… It's the same thing. It's just more steps.

00:37:50.080 --> 00:37:52.080
It's transparency with more steps. Yeah.

00:37:52.080 --> 00:37:57.080
And more steps is bad, because that makes it harder for the users.

00:37:57.080 --> 00:38:01.080
It makes it easier for people to get confused or to get scammed or tricked.

00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:06.285
So, anyway, I agree with you, or I agree with what you were saying,

00:38:06.285 --> 00:38:12.000
that transparency has its uses. Yeah. And also, just in general,

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:15.080
like, if the users… I really like a certain kind of humility.

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:22.642
Yeah. Like, if I think 80% of the people out there should behave differently than they

00:38:22.642 --> 00:38:28.580
do… Yeah. It's like, "I'm not wrong. The children are wrong." Right?

00:38:28.580 --> 00:38:34.160
Like, maybe they know something I don't about their lives and their priorities and their situation.

00:38:34.160 --> 00:38:42.160
Yeah. Second question is, regarding Crosslink, I mean, as I'm guessing you're well aware,

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:45.960
there's been a lot of back and forth around that in the community of people.

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:51.120
People in favor of people against it, like, you know, we were speaking earlier in this

00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:55.520
conversation about, like, some critiques and the case for it,

00:38:55.520 --> 00:39:02.960
obviously. What's the realistic political path for Crosslink to get to Mainnet?

00:39:02.960 --> 00:39:06.960
Do you have any timeline? What are the next plans, the roadmap?

00:39:06.960 --> 00:39:10.960
Yeah. I don't know about the political future.

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:12.960
Like, how will people's feelings change?

00:39:12.960 --> 00:39:19.960
I can guess or speculate. I mean, one thing I really think is coin prices influence people's feelings.

00:39:19.960 --> 00:39:23.760
Yeah. Yeah, for sure. Like, when the price is going up, everyone says,

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:25.960
"Don't change anything and ruin it." It's working.

00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:29.960
Don't ruin it. It's working. Yeah. Like, don't touch it.

00:39:29.960 --> 00:39:31.960
And when the price is going down, everyone says, "Devs, do something." Yes.

00:39:31.960 --> 00:39:37.862
And also, a lot of the opposition to Crosslink and a lot of the other recent,

00:39:37.862 --> 00:39:43.503
like, governance and strategic and mimetic debates within Zcash have been about comparing

00:39:43.503 --> 00:39:44.840
Zcash to Bitcoin.

00:39:44.840 --> 00:39:47.840
Yeah. Yeah. And so, that whole movement was the Zcash equals encrypted Bitcoin movement.

00:39:47.840 --> 00:39:51.707
Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. And so, that's where I think, as long as the Bitcoin price languishes,

00:39:51.707 --> 00:39:54.465
that movement will lose steam. Yeah. It'll be less and less exciting to people to be

00:39:54.465 --> 00:39:55.840
like encrypted Bitcoin if Bitcoin is not going up.

00:39:55.840 --> 00:39:58.590
Yeah. Yeah. If Bitcoin starts going up again, then everyone's going to start saying,

00:39:58.590 --> 00:40:01.840
"Let's be more like Bitcoin." Yeah. But, okay.

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:03.840
So, I don't know about politics. Hard to predict.

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:05.673
Whatever. What we're doing, spending a lot of effort on, and I'm excited about how I

00:40:05.673 --> 00:40:06.840
think it will be also useful for the future.

00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:15.998
Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. And I'm excited about how I think it will be also

00:40:15.998 --> 00:40:18.720
useful for Tachyon and other things in the future.

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:28.138
Yeah. Is assume you don't know about politics, assume there's conflicting opinions or

00:40:28.138 --> 00:40:36.464
opinions change over time, but you can develop and test and get people to start using

00:40:36.464 --> 00:40:41.660
the thing. Yeah. The more you do that, then the more knowledge and understanding people

00:40:41.660 --> 00:40:48.435
have, the more mature the thing is, and the more you and others can judge what are the

00:40:48.435 --> 00:40:54.140
actual trade-offs of this. Yeah. There might or might not be a future.

00:40:54.140 --> 00:40:55.680
So that's what we're currently doing with Crosslink.

00:40:55.680 --> 00:40:58.660
We've got this prototype that we've implemented.

00:40:58.660 --> 00:41:03.940
It's very buggy, which is very good opportunity to find bugs and learn how things can

00:41:03.940 --> 00:41:11.200
go wrong. Yeah. And we've got a very active community of developers who are building

00:41:11.200 --> 00:41:15.640
their own block servers and patches and stuff.

00:41:15.640 --> 00:41:18.540
And just users who want to run it and see how it runs.

00:41:18.540 --> 00:41:23.360
So there's a really good feedback loop, both we're learning from them and they're learning

00:41:23.360 --> 00:41:25.940
from Shield Labs, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:41:25.940 --> 00:41:31.053
I think that's a really good process. In the past, we had the opposite process in Zcash,

00:41:31.053 --> 00:41:35.120
which was like the whole community will decide if this is a good idea.

00:41:35.120 --> 00:41:39.280
Having made that decision, then some developers will go off for the next few years and

00:41:39.280 --> 00:41:42.920
implement the thing. Yeah. And that's the wrong way around.

00:41:42.920 --> 00:41:48.160
And a good example is ZSAs. Like three years ago, the entire Zcash community,

00:41:48.160 --> 00:41:50.940
except for me, said, "That's a great idea.

00:41:50.940 --> 00:41:55.329
We should definitely prioritize that as one of the most important priorities." So now

00:41:55.329 --> 00:41:59.413
a bunch of developers have spent three years and millions of dollars implementing that

00:41:59.413 --> 00:42:06.913
thing, at which point some faction or one-thirds or two-thirds or whatever of the Zcash

00:42:06.913 --> 00:42:10.420
community said, "Actually, we don't want that thing." Yeah.

00:42:10.420 --> 00:42:13.460
Yeah. It's too late and too early.

00:42:13.460 --> 00:42:17.420
We don't even know how ZSAs would work in practice, because no one's ever implemented

00:42:17.420 --> 00:42:23.502
a wallet and ever, to my knowledge, maybe someone has, but I've never seen a wallet that

00:42:23.502 --> 00:42:27.271
could actually use a ZSA for anything. But it's also too late,

00:42:27.271 --> 00:42:31.060
because we've already spent millions of dollars and many years of skilled teams' time

00:42:31.060 --> 00:42:35.049
to implement it. Anyway, so we're trying to do things in the other order with Crosslink,

00:42:35.049 --> 00:42:39.624
where you implement it first, you can see how it works, what it does and doesn't do,

00:42:39.624 --> 00:42:44.127
what the trade-offs are, and then either the Zcash community changes their -- well,

00:42:44.127 --> 00:42:47.230
the ones who currently oppose it change their minds and say,

00:42:47.230 --> 00:42:52.065
"Actually, that doesn't look so bad," and then we could collectively -- the whole Zcash

00:42:52.065 --> 00:42:57.825
ecosystem and community could upgrade the central main net to add Crosslink,

00:42:57.825 --> 00:43:05.360
especially because we designed it to be very modular and sort of deployable.

00:43:05.360 --> 00:43:08.866
Or if they're still not into it, then we have this interesting situation where you've

00:43:08.866 --> 00:43:11.880
got this like... It's kind of like a sidechain.

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:19.180
It doesn't have the economic linkage to real Zec, but it has the community and the functionality.

00:43:19.180 --> 00:43:26.160
I don't know. There may be some way to keep it growing on the side.

00:43:26.160 --> 00:43:32.520
Anyway, I wouldn't be surprised if this same sort of process turns out to be good for Tachyon.

00:43:32.520 --> 00:43:39.703
Yeah. Yeah. Speaking of the sidechain, I think one of the proposals someone has floated

00:43:39.703 --> 00:43:44.920
around was the idea of having some sort of cannery network, as they call it,

00:43:44.920 --> 00:43:50.977
sort of like Polkadot or Kusama I think it's called, where we can kind of like go crazy

00:43:50.977 --> 00:43:54.320
and like -- I mean, go crazy with a certain bounce, right?

00:43:54.320 --> 00:43:59.540
Right. Like move fast and break things in a safe space.

00:43:59.540 --> 00:44:04.660
So that we can be maybe more conservative on the main chain and then only really implement

00:44:04.660 --> 00:44:07.860
like the really very solid stuff. Right.

00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:09.860
Yeah. That's a very interesting proposal in my opinion.

00:44:09.860 --> 00:44:11.820
Yeah. And I think that's one of the best turnstiles.

00:44:11.820 --> 00:44:17.163
One of my other favorite technologies, also underappreciated for many years,

00:44:17.163 --> 00:44:22.140
but now people appreciate them because they've saved our bacon multiple times.

00:44:22.140 --> 00:44:29.640
And it's one of those Zcash innovations that I hope other cryptocurrencies and technologies

00:44:29.640 --> 00:44:35.900
will sort of start -- I think they'll eventually realize once they have the hard experience

00:44:35.900 --> 00:44:40.260
themselves of, "Oh, if only we had a turnstile here, wouldn't that be great?" Yes.

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:44.273
And turnstiles like these side chains and this canary network,

00:44:44.273 --> 00:44:48.101
it's like you could imagine hypothetically -- I don't know how this would work,

00:44:48.101 --> 00:44:53.420
but you can imagine we're going to make a pool that is going to be interoperable with

00:44:53.420 --> 00:44:58.720
the Zcash main chain, but this is a dangerous, risky technology.

00:44:58.720 --> 00:45:01.700
So it has a turnstile and there's like a limit.

00:45:01.700 --> 00:45:05.500
You can't have more than 10,000 Zcash in this pool at a time.

00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:10.140
Yeah. Interesting. Or you can't move more than 100 Zcash a day into this pool.

00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:14.366
Or something like that. Then if this one has a catastrophic failure,

00:45:14.366 --> 00:45:18.140
it's not going to hurt everyone else who chose to not risk it.

00:45:18.140 --> 00:45:21.140
Yeah, the exposure is limited. Yeah. It's interesting.

00:45:21.140 --> 00:45:23.140
I don't know. There's a lot of possibilities for the future.

00:45:23.140 --> 00:45:26.300
Yeah. It's definitely something that should be considered.

00:45:26.300 --> 00:45:32.200
For sure. I agree. Yeah. Last question is for the future of Zcash.

00:45:32.200 --> 00:45:33.200
What's your vision?

00:45:33.200 --> 00:45:40.020
I know you wrote this blog post, was it a year or two ago, about like honoring the holder.

00:45:40.020 --> 00:45:46.480
What's your vision of Zcash for the next decade or so?

00:45:46.480 --> 00:45:51.993
How do you envision Zcash? I know a lot of people have now started really thinking of

00:45:51.993 --> 00:45:57.860
Zcash as a store of value. But I mean, there's like cash in the name Zcash.

00:45:57.860 --> 00:46:04.550
So have you a particular opinion about that, about the use cases,

00:46:04.550 --> 00:46:07.780
about your vision of Zcash?

00:46:07.780 --> 00:46:14.377
Has that changed? I think of... I don't really separate money into store of value and

00:46:14.377 --> 00:46:18.459
medium of exchange. I think it's like two sides of the same coin or whatever,

00:46:18.459 --> 00:46:23.340
the chicken and the egg. It's really the same thing.

00:46:23.340 --> 00:46:35.083
My vision is Zcash continues to grow in distribution, users,

00:46:35.083 --> 00:46:40.780
and security and sustainability. I think it's the same thing.

00:46:40.780 --> 00:46:43.655
I think it's the same kind of thing.
